Attribution for the ToDo lists (brief §3.5 and §3.6). The status machinery itself is generic and lives in [[$:/plugins/tiddlywiki/forms/todo]]; what is NHN-specific is deciding which service an item belongs to.

!! Which service a review is about

`nhn-review-service` needs two attempts, because the data answers the question two ways:

# the **service-name tag**, when that tag has a tiddler behind it that is itself tagged a service type (`nhn-servicename`);
# otherwise the name **parsed out of the title**, `NN <Tjeneste> - hovedtrekk og endringer …`, but //only if it is exactly a member of `nhn-services`// — not merely something carrying a service-type tag, which would let an under-tagged non-service (no business unit, or itself a month-prefixed review) absorb the attribution.

The second is not a guess. 174 of the 781 reviews carry no recognisable service tag, and for 14 of them the title names a service that plainly exists — without the fallback those 14 would be reported as missing every month, which is the fastest way to make people stop believing a ToDo list. The remaining 160 name services that have no tiddler at all (`Kjernejournal` is a tag, not a tiddler), so they belong to no row and are left alone rather than guessed at.

Objectives need no such fallback: their titles follow no convention, so `nhn-servicename` is used directly and an objective with no service tag simply belongs to no row.

!! Other pieces

* `nhn-template-text` — the `mal` tiddler, which the review ToDo passes as `todo-template`. OKRs have no template, so that list passes nothing. The review ToDo also passes `nhn-review-seed-of` as `todo-seed`, because a new review is the template //plus// its service's Power BI link (see [[$:/plugins/intertwingled-innovations/nhn/powerbi]]).
* `nhn-result-count` — how many `Resultat` hang off an objective. Not part of the status, but 39% of the 2026 objectives have none, which is worth showing next to one that "exists".

!! Reviews that are still a retired template

When the `mal` tiddler is reworded, a review created from the old wording and never written no longer matches the template, so the review ToDo counts it as written. `nhn-reviews-retired-template` lists those reviews for [[Anomalier]]: every review (archived ones included, as in the ToDo lists) whose text, trimmed, is exactly the text of a retired template.

A retired template is a tiddler tagged `$:/tags/nhn/RetiredTemplate` holding the old wording verbatim. The plugin ships the one NHN replaced in September 2026 ([[$:/plugins/intertwingled-innovations/nhn/retired-templates/review-2026-01]]). The next time the template changes, copy the outgoing text into another such tiddler; nothing else needs editing.

The list is also the check to run //before// swapping the template in a wiki: with the plugin installed and the old `mal` still in place, it names every review that the swap would turn from «Kun mal» into written.

!! Lists glued to a paragraph

`nhn-glued-lists` — wikitext tiddlers in which a `*` or `#` list line directly follows a line of paragraph text with no blank line between. TiddlyWiki then renders the bullets as literal text inside the paragraph. Lines that are themselves block-level (headings, tables, other list items, definitions, quotes, rules, HTML, code fences, pragmas) are legitimate predecessors and are not reported.

!! Tags that differ only in casing

`nhn-tag-casing-drift` — every tag for which another tag exists with the same letters and different capitalisation, both variants listed, most-used last. Computed rather than enumerated: a fixed table of known variants was what the page carried before, and it went on reporting six `tjeneste` spellings while `Resultat`/`resultat` and `MyHealth@EU`/`Myhealth@EU` drifted past it unmentioned. Drift is not a fixed list — it is whatever people have typed since — so the page has to derive it from the tags that are actually in use.

`nhn-tag-casing-canonical` names the variant to keep: the one used by the most tiddlers. It is what the merge action below normalises onto by default, which turns the listing from a complaint into a proposal the reader can accept with one click.

`nhn-all-tags` is the distinct tags in use, shared by both. It is rescanned per tag, so the listing costs roughly half a second on this corpus — acceptable on a page opened to audit the data, and not used anywhere in the everyday views.

!! Merging the variants

[[Anomalier]] does not only report the drift, it offers to fix it. The merge itself is generic and lives in the engine ([[$:/plugins/tiddlywiki/forms/merge]]); what these functions add is the //family// — the set of variants a merge acts on, and which of them to keep.

`nhn-tag-casing-families` — one representative tag per drifting family, so the page renders one merge control per concept rather than one row per variant. The representative is the family's canonical variant, which is stable for as long as the family exists and disappears with it once merged.

Its `drift` parameter is an optimisation, not configuration: `nhn-tag-casing-drift` costs about half a second here — it is the one expensive thing on the page — and calling it once for the heading count and again for the families would double that. Passing the list in lets [[Anomalier]] compute it once; called with nothing it computes its own, so the function still stands alone.

`nhn-tag-casing-variants` — every spelling of the family the current tiddler belongs to, most-used first. Called with the family as `currentTiddler`, but any variant of the family gives the same answer: membership is case-insensitive equality, which is symmetric.

`nhn-tag-merge-target` is the variant the merge will keep, and `nhn-tag-merge-losers` the ones it will remove. The target defaults to `nhn-tag-casing-canonical` — the most-used spelling — but the page lets it be chosen, because most-used is a heuristic and not always the correct one: `Ekstern Tjeneste` outnumbers `Ekstern tjeneste` more than four to one while the `Styring …` tags and the rest of the taxonomy spell it in lower case. The choice is held in `nhn-tag-merge-state`, a `$:/state` tiddler per family, and is intersected back against the variants actually in use so that a stale choice falls back to the canonical rather than merging onto a tag that no longer exists.
